{{else}}{{if .Builtin}}## Design rules (strict)

1. **Readability first**: `--text-primary` must contrast strongly with `--bg-start`/`--bg-end` (WCAG AA intuition). Dark backgrounds get light text; light backgrounds get dark text.
2. **The dark flag**: when the background is overall dark (deep purple, navy, black), set `"dark": true` and use a translucent dark `--surface` (e.g. `rgba(55,50,120,0.75)`) with light text throughout; otherwise `"dark": false` with a translucent white surface (e.g. `rgba(255,255,255,0.72)`).
3. **Harmonious gradient**: `--bg-start` and `--bg-end` should be a soft transition within one color family — never clashing hues.
4. **Restrained status colors**: `--success`/`--warning`/`--error` keep their semantic hues (green / amber / red); restyle only lightness and saturation.
5. **Values must be color literals only**: hex (`#rrggbb` / `#rrggbbaa`), `rgb()/rgba()`, `hsl()/hsla()`. No other CSS syntax (url, gradient functions, var references, …).
6. **Provide all 12 variables** — no omissions.
7. Give the theme a fitting short name (1–4 words, one emoji allowed).

{{else}}## Design rules (strict)

1. **Provide every variable listed above — not one fewer, not one more.** Keys that were not listed are discarded; keys that are missing leave this frontend rendering incompletely.
2. **Every value must match the kind noted beside its variable.** The permitted shape is written next to the kind; follow it — no lengths where a color is asked for, no percent signs where a ratio is.
3. **Readability first**: text variables must contrast strongly with background variables (WCAG AA intuition). Dark grounds get light text; light grounds get dark text.
4. **One theme, one character**: colors within a single family or a deliberate complement; numeric variables (radius, blur, spacing) keep one personality across the set — all restrained or all bold, not a mix.
5. **The dark flag**: `true` when the whole thing reads dark, `false` otherwise.
6. **Literals only** — no `url()`, `var()`, `calc()`, or any other CSS syntax.
7. Give the theme a fitting short name (1–4 words, one emoji allowed).

